## Context

The [original incident page](/kb/incidents/4471/) calls all reports one parser bug.
The [old triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe/) says to merge matching subjects without
reading bodies. Its live example disagrees with that instruction. The decision behind
that rule was accepted on 11 June 2024. Keep
[decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject/) unchanged as a historical record.
This new record proposes replacing it, not rewriting its history.

The desk serves every product, not only `sched-prod`. A shared symptom such as
"wrong date" does not establish a shared cause, within one product or across products.

## Engineering update, 5 October 2026

The support desk's engineering update on the
[course challenge page](https://hq.mega.dev/challenges/what-it-shows), read on
31 October 2026, says that original ticket #4471 closed on 5 October:

- The parser is **fixed**, with the change subject
  `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. Its
  [tracking ticket #2](https://github.com/evilbc/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2)
  is closed as completed.
- The hint is **won't fix** because the `booking-v3` form was retired. Its
  [tracking ticket #1](https://github.com/evilbc/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1)
  is closed as not planned and labeled `wontfix`. Retirement is not a hint fix.

The export predates this update. Counts, reproductions, and the original ticket
assignments below describe that historical snapshot. Engineering supplied a fix
subject, not a public patch or commit SHA. We record its stated result without
claiming to have implemented or independently verified the production change.

## What #4471 contains

These are computed from the deduplicated report IDs, the tenant directory, and the
export's `causeOf` function. Browser locale and `meta.intakeTag` are intake guesses,
not the tenant's date convention.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The original ticket contains two known mechanisms. The cause table describes the
old ticket system: its warning that the hint problem has no ticket refers to that
export. The two linked GitHub tickets below track this investigation.

### Shared parser reads month first

The shared parser treats date input as month first even when the tenant writes day
first. For a day-first tenant, entering `07/04/2026` for 7 April produces 4 July.
`R-41200` and its correction `R-41201` show this reversal. They are separate report
IDs, so both count. `R-41205` is another example: its French tenant uses day-first
dates even though intake recorded an American browser locale and tagged it `hint`.

Fix parsing against the tenant's explicit date convention, or accept an unambiguous
format. Preserve month-first behavior for month-first tenants. Cover app, email, and
the older booking form; a change to the newer form's hint cannot fix those paths.
Do not silently guess when a tenant is unknown.

The parser ticket and its exact report membership are in
[GitHub issue #2](https://github.com/evilbc/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2).
Its count includes the unresolved timing reports listed below. That is the export's
classification, not proof that the parser caused every body in the group.

### New booking form gives a month-first hint to day-first tenants

For `booking-v3`, the date field prints `MM/DD/YYYY`. A day-first tenant's form
interprets the input using the tenant's convention. Following the printed hint can
therefore reverse the date. `R-41230`, `R-41241`, and `R-41251` come through this
form at Copper Studio, Alder Physio, and Pale Harbour respectively.

For example, on Alder Physio's `de-DE` form, a person following the hint types
`04/07/2026` for 7 April. A day-first form reads that as 4 July. The expected
behavior is a hint that matches the actual parser and the tenant locale.

Fix the displayed hint and validate its agreement with form parsing. This is a
separate change from the shared parser fix. Track it in
[GitHub issue #1](https://github.com/evilbc/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1).
Other channels at these same tenants can still belong to the parser issue.

## Check the evidence before counting

<ReportAudit />

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

The report bodies also describe clients attending the wrong day, duplicate
meetings, manual date changes, and people replacing the product with phone calls.
Those are user costs, not proof of extra technical causes.

## Proposed triage decision

Deduplicate reports only when the evidence supports the same underlying problem
in the same product and affected behavior. Record the tenant, its locale, form
version, channel, expected date, observed date, and any reproducible trigger.
Read the report body. Use the subject to find candidates, never to decide a merge.
Use browser locale and intake tags as clues, never as the final classification.

The same subject can hide different causes, and different subjects can describe
the same cause. Unknown tenants and unexplained behavior stay separate for
investigation rather than being merged by default.

<DedupeExplorer />

The explorer evaluates the two known date causes, not every possible product
failure. For unknown tenants it returns no cause and refuses to call them duplicates.
Do not extend this date-specific classifier into a desk-wide rule.

## Follow-up and limits

The two tracking tickets record different closure outcomes, with the engineering
update cited in each body. Keep report IDs attached so future exports can update
counts without double counting. Reproduce the timing anomalies before assigning
their causes; the parser fix's subject alone does not establish their resolution.
Changing this documentation does not move reports in the support system.
A desk owner must accept the new triage decision before it becomes policy.

Sources are the original export's `reports-4471.json`, `desk-notes.json`, tenant
JSON files, `causes.ts`, `locales.ts`, `CauseTable.svelte`, `QueueTimeline.svelte`,
and `DedupeExplorer.svelte`. All remain in this repository.

